  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Lithium Knowledge · Practical · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>
      <h1>How to Store Lithium Batteries</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The best way to store a lithium battery contradicts the instinct to charge it first. The storage-optimal
        state is half-full, cool and untouched.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Store lithium batteries at <strong>40–60% state of charge</strong>, in a
        <strong>cool, stable place</strong>. Both full and empty accelerate
        <a href="/lithium/lifespan/">calendar aging</a>, and heat roughly doubles the aging rate every 10°C — which
        is why devices ship at half charge and why hot garages are the worst storage spot.
      </div>

      <h2>How to store, step by step</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Set the state of charge</strong> — bring the battery to ~40–60% before long storage, not full and not empty.</li>
        <li><strong>Pick a cool, stable spot</strong> — room temperature or below, away from direct heat and sunlight.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose a dry, safe location</strong> — away from flammables and anything that could short the terminals.</li>
        <li><strong>Disconnect or isolate</strong> — remove the load (and, if possible, the BMS draw) so nothing drains it while it sits.</li>
        <li><strong>Recheck periodically</strong> — every few months for long storage, confirm the SOC is still near the target.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>How should lithium batteries be stored?</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Storage-optimal</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>State of charge</strong></td><td>~40–60%</td><td>Minimises electrode stress at both extremes</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Temperature</strong></td><td>Cool, stable (~15–25°C)</td><td>Heat accelerates every aging reaction</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Environment</strong></td><td>Dry, away from flammables</td><td>Damaged cells can vent or burn</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Why half-charge, not full</h2>
      <p>
        Full charge keeps the cathode at its highest voltage — the state where side reactions proceed fastest —
        while deep-empty risks over-discharge and cell damage. The middle range sits in the quiet zone between
        the two stresses, which is why manufacturers ship cells at partial charge and why long-term storage
        guidance centres on it (see <a href="/lithium/soc-vs-soh/">SOC</a> for the state-of-charge language).
      </p>

      <h2>The heat rule</h2>
      <p>
        Heat is the other multiplier: storage in a hot car or garage can age a battery several times faster than
        a cool shelf. Combined with partial charge, cool storage is the single biggest lever on a stored battery's
        life — more than any brand choice (see
        <a href="/lead-acid/self-discharge/">self-discharge</a> for the lead-acid version of the same physics).
      </p>
      <h2>Storage state of charge by duration</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Storage length</th><th>Recommended SOC</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Days to a few weeks</td><td>40–80%</td><td>Anything but full and hot</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Months</td><td>40–60%</td><td>The sweet spot — check periodically</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Very long (12+ months)</td><td>~50%</td><td>Recheck SOC every few months</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted">The pattern is one rule: partial charge, cool, and a periodic check — the three levers that minimise calendar aging.</p>


      <h2>Our Read</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>My take:</strong> The lithium storage rule is counterintuitive but cheap — half charge, cool
          shelf — and it matters because storage damage is invisible: the battery that aged badly in a hot drawer
          fails later, with no warning at the time.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>The reasoning:</strong> Calendar aging runs silently, driven by charge state and temperature. The two
          rules — middle charge, cool place — attack both drivers directly and cost nothing, which makes them the
          highest-return storage advice in batteries.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>My editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>What charge should lithium batteries be stored at?</h3>
      <p>Around 40-60% state of charge. Full charge and empty both age the battery faster in storage — the middle range minimises the stress on the electrodes, which is why devices ship at roughly half charge.</p>
      <h3>What temperature should lithium batteries be stored at?</h3>
      <p>Cool and stable — room temperature or below. Heat roughly doubles the aging rate every 10°C, so a hot garage or car interior is the worst place for a stored lithium battery.</p>
<h2>What buyers should ask</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Will the battery be stored at the correct partial charge (around 40–60%), not full or empty?</li>
  <li>Is the storage location cool, since heat accelerates calendar aging during storage?</li>
  <li>How long is the storage period, and does the state-of-charge need a periodic top-up?</li>
  <li>Is the battery disconnected and protected from any slow parasitic load during storage?</li>
</ul>
<h2>The bottom line</h2>
<p>Lithium batteries store best at partial charge and cool — around 40–60% state of charge, away from heat — not full and not empty. Set the storage charge, keep it cool, and the battery survives the idle period with most of its life intact.</p>

<h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>Cross-verified from cell-manufacturer storage guidance and battery engineering references.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Cell-manufacturer documentation — storage state-of-charge and temperature guidance.</li>
        <li>Battery engineering references — calendar aging dependence on SOC and temperature.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-16</p>
    </div>
  </section>
